        "content": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n<title>Quantum mechanics - Wikipedia</title>\n<script>\nwindow.MathJax = {\n  tex: { inlineMath: [['$', '$'], ['\\\\(', '\\\\)']], displayMath: [['$$', '$$'], ['\\\\[', '\\\\]']] },\n  svg: { fontCache: 'global' }\n};\n</script>\n<script src=\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mathjax@3/es5/tex-svg.js\"></script>\n<style>\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  body {\n    margin: 0;\n    background: #fff;\n    color: #050505;\n    font-family: Georgia, \"Times New Roman\", serif;\n  }\n  .page {\n    width: 1530px;\n    min-height: 1980px;\n    margin: 0 auto;\n    padding: 36px 59px 34px 88px;\n    position: relative;\n  }\n  .topline {\n    height: 35px;\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 20px;\n    position: relative;\n  }\n  .date { position: absolute; left: -29px; top: 1px; }\n  .doc-title { text-align: center; padding-left: 5px; }\n  h1 {\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 37px;\n    letter-spacing: .1px;\n    line-height: 1.12;\n    margin: 4px 0 14px 0;\n    font-weight: 700;\n  }\n  p {\n    font-size: 32px;\n    line-height: 1.25;\n    margin: 0 0 32px 0;\n  }\n  .lead-area {\n    overflow: hidden;\n    margin-bottom: 29px;\n  }\n  .lead {\n    width: 681px;\n    float: left;\n    text-align: justify;\n    margin: 0;\n  }\n  .figure {\n    float: right;\n    width: 621px;\n    margin: 10px 35px 0 0;\n  }\n  .placeholder {\n    width: 621px;\n    height: 380px;\n    border: 1px solid #d3d3d3;\n    position: relative;\n    overflow: hidden;\n    color: #444;\n    background: #fff;\n  }\n  .placeholder .caption {\n    position: absolute;\n    left: 7px;\n    bottom: 7px;\n    font: 14px Arial, sans-serif;\n    color: #777;\n  }\n  .diagram-line {\n    position: absolute;\n    height: 4px;\n    background: #ed2727;\n    transform-origin: left center;\n  }\n  .beam {\n    width: 73px;\n    height: 73px;\n    border: 3px solid #555;\n    position: absolute;\n    transform: rotate(0deg);\n  }\n  .beam:after {\n    content: \"\";\n    position: absolute;\n    width: 95px;\n    height: 3px;\n    top: 32px;\n    left: -13px;\n    background: #111;\n    transform: rotate(-45deg);\n  }\n  .phase {\n    position: absolute;\n    left: 248px;\n    top: 106px;\n    width: 96px;\n    height: 40px;\n    background: #7d7d7d;\n    border: 2px solid #4b4b4b;\n  }\n  .diagram-label {\n    position: absolute;\n    font-size: 27px;\n    color: #333;\n    white-space: nowrap;\n  }\n  .figure-caption {\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 26px;\n    color: #676767;\n    line-height: 1.2;\n    margin: 8px 0 0 4px;\n    white-space: nowrap;\n  }\n  .body-text { clear: both; }\n  .indent-math {\n    margin: -2px 0 29px 48px;\n    font-size: 31px;\n    line-height: 1.2;\n  }\n  .matrixpara { margin-bottom: 30px; }\n  .display {\n    margin: 6px 0 20px 48px;\n    font-size: 32px;\n    line-height: 1.1;\n  }\n  .prob-intro { margin-bottom: 10px; }\n  .prob {\n    margin: 2px 0 21px 48px;\n    font-size: 32px;\n  }\n  .final { margin-top: 4px; }\n  .under {\n    text-decoration: underline;\n    text-decoration-color: #8d8d8d;\n    text-underline-offset: 5px;\n  }\n  .footer {\n    position: absolute;\n    bottom: 36px;\n    left: 59px;\n    right: 59px;\n    font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;\n    font-size: 20px;\n  }\n  .page-no { float: right; }\n</style>\n</head>\n<body>\n<div class=\"page\">\n  <div class=\"topline\">\n    <span class=\"date\">2026/6/29 03:00</span>\n    <div class=\"doc-title\">Quantum mechanics - Wikipedia</div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h1>Mach–Zehnder interferometer</h1>\n\n  <div class=\"lead-area\">\n    <p class=\"lead\">\n      The <span class=\"under\">Mach–Zehnder</span> interferometer (MZI)<br>\n      illustrates the concepts of superposition and<br>\n      interference with linear algebra in dimension 2,<br>\n      rather than differential equations. It can be seen as<br>\n      a simplified version of the double-slit experiment,<br>\n      but it is of interest in its own right, for example in<br>\n      the <span class=\"under\">delayed choice quantum eraser</span>, the <span class=\"under\">Elitzur–</span><br>\n      <span class=\"under\">Vaidman bomb tester</span>, and in studies of quantum<br>\n      entanglement.<span class=\"under\">[36][37]</span>\n    </p>\n\n    <div class=\"figure\">\n      <div class=\"placeholder\" aria-label=\"Diagram: Mach–Zehnder interferometer schematic\">\n        <div class=\"diagram-line\" style=\"left:0;top:336px;width:181px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-line\" style=\"left:143px;top:126px;width:4px;height:213px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-line\" style=\"left:143px;top:126px;width:307px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-line\" style=\"left:448px;top:1px;width:4px;height:338px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-line\" style=\"left:448px;top:126px;width:132px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"beam\" style=\"left:109px;top:302px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"beam\" style=\"left:109px;top:91px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"beam\" style=\"left:414px;top:91px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"beam\" style=\"left:414px;top:302px;\"></div>\n        <div class=\"phase\"></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-label\" style=\"left:278px;top:144px;\">ΔΦ</div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-label\" style=\"left:315px;top:14px;\">~ sin² <span style=\"font-size:22px;\">ΔΦ</span><span style=\"font-size:16px;vertical-align:sub;\">2</span></div>\n        <div class=\"diagram-label\" style=\"left:500px;top:151px;\">~ cos² <span style=\"font-size:22px;\">ΔΦ</span><span style=\"font-size:16px;vertical-align:sub;\">2</span></div>\n        <span class=\"caption\">[Diagram: Mach–Zehnder interferometer schematic]</span>\n      </div>\n      <div class=\"figure-caption\">Schematic of a Mach–Zehnder interferometer</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"body-text\">\n    <p>\n      We can model a photon going through the<br>\n      interferometer by considering that at each point it<br>\n      can be in a superposition of only two paths: the<br>\n      \"lower\" path which starts from the left, goes straight through both beam splitters, and ends at the top,<br>\n      and the \"upper\" path which starts from the bottom, goes straight through both beam splitters, and<br>\n      ends at the right. The quantum state of the photon is therefore a vector $\\psi\\in\\mathbb{C}^{2}$ that is a superposition\n    </p>\n\n    <div class=\"indent-math\">\n      of the \"lower\" path $\\psi_l=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$ and the \"upper\" path $\\psi_u=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$, that is, $\\psi=\\alpha\\psi_l+\\beta\\psi_u$ for<br>\n      complex $\\alpha,\\beta$. In order to respect the postulate that $\\langle\\psi,\\psi\\rangle=1$ we require that $|\\alpha|^2+|\\beta|^2=1$.\n    </div>\n\n    <p class=\"matrixpara\">\n      Both <span class=\"under\">beam splitters</span> are modelled as the unitary matrix $B=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&i\\\\i&1\\end{pmatrix}$, which means that when a<br>\n      photon meets the beam splitter it will either stay on the same path with a probability amplitude of<br>\n      $1/\\sqrt2$, or be reflected to the other path with a probability amplitude of $i/\\sqrt2$. The phase shifter on the<br>\n      upper arm is modelled as the unitary matrix $P=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&e^{i\\Delta\\Phi}\\end{pmatrix}$, which means that if the photon is on<br>\n      the \"upper\" path it will gain a relative phase of $\\Delta\\Phi$, and it will stay unchanged if it is in the lower path.\n    </p>\n\n    <p>\n      A photon that enters the interferometer from the left will then be acted upon with a beam splitter $B$, a<br>\n      phase shifter $P$, and another beam splitter $B$, and so end up in the state\n    </p>\n\n    <div class=\"display\">\n      $$BPB\\psi_l=ie^{i\\Delta\\Phi/2}\\begin{pmatrix}-\\sin(\\Delta\\Phi/2)\\\\ \\cos(\\Delta\\Phi/2)\\end{pmatrix},$$\n    </div>\n\n    <p class=\"prob-intro\">and the probabilities that it will be detected at the right or at the top are given respectively by</p>\n\n    <div class=\"prob\">\n      $$p(u)=|\\langle\\psi_u,BPB\\psi_l\\rangle|^2=\\cos^2\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{2},$$\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"prob\">\n      $$p(l)=|\\langle\\psi_l,BPB\\psi_l\\rangle|^2=\\sin^2\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{2}.$$\n    </div>\n\n    <p class=\"final\">\n      One can therefore use the Mach–Zehnder interferometer to estimate the <span class=\"under\">phase shift</span> by estimating<br>\n      these probabilities.\n    </p>\n  </div>\n\n  <div class=\"footer\">\n    <span>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_mechanics</span>\n    <span class=\"page-no\">11/32</span>\n  </div>\n</div>\n</body>\n</html>"
